  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure builds through the region today.\n\nBroad high pressure is centered over central Canada north of the\nGreat Lakes with a second center over western New York and\nwestern Pennsylvania. High pressure builds east today, and one\ncenter of the high will be along the Canada/New York border with\nthe second center offshore, east of New Jersey and south of Long\nIsland.\n\nThe weather will be fair today with highs in the 80s to near 90\ninland and cooler at the coasts. Humidity levels will be fairly\ncomfortable for most northern and inland areas where dew points\nwill be in the 50s to around 60, and a touch humid for Delmarva\nand southern New Jersey where dew points will be in the mid and\nupper 60s.\n\nAn upper trough will be over the northeast, and some weak\nshortwave energy will pass through the region and will interact\nwith low level moisture and a surface sea breeze to touch off a\nfew weak showers across extreme southern New Jersey and portions\nof Delaware.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather returns Sunday into Monday.\n\nOur next system arrives Sunday into Monday as a low pressure \nsystem tracks from near the Great Lakes region into the \nNortheast bringing showers and thunderstorms to the area. \n\nThere continues to remain variations among guidance in terms of\nthe exact timing of precipitation. Right now, the general trend\nis by mid to late morning on Sunday, precipitation is moving \ninto the area. The coverage increases into the afternoon and \nevening as warm air advection sets up allowing for an increase \nin ascent across the region. Additional showers and \nthunderstorms carry over into Sunday night. By Monday, we trend \ndrier through the day. Although, some lingering chances for \nprecipitation will remain before we truly dry out by Monday \nnight as a high pressure system builds in. \n\nRegardless of the exact timing, the primary threat Sunday into \nSunday night is heavy rainfall leading to the risk of flash \nflooding. There continues to be a signal among model guidance \nshowing PWAT values surging into the 2-2.5 inch range Sunday \ninto Sunday night. This combined with warm cloud layer processes\nshown in model soundings, and strengthen flow at 850mb, all \nshowcase the risk for heavy rainfall, especially with any \ntraining that occurs. As a result, WPC has our whole area in a \nSlight Risk (level 2 out of 4) in the Excessive Rainfall Outlook\nfor Sunday into Sunday night with rainfall rates upwards of 2-3\ninches per hour possible.\n\nOne aspect that remains a bit more uncertain is the threat for \nsevere thunderstorms. The extent of instability is a key part \nthat remains uncertain given cloud cover and rounds of \nprecipitation through the day Sunday. However, there continues \nto be the trend of increasing shear and upper-level forcing for \nascent from synoptic scale features. SPC has areas south of \nPhiladelphia highlighted in a Marginal Risk (level 1 out of 5) \nfor isolated severe thunderstorms with the primary concern being\ndamaging wind gusts.",
